<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sertorian State</h1><p class="st-meta">80 BCE – 72 BCE · capital Osca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The breakaway Roman regime of Quintus Sertorius, who with Iberian allies held most of Hispania against the Senate for nearly a decade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a>, a supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Marius</a>, was driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla's</a> forces but returned in 80 BCE at the invitation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lusitanians">Lusitanians</a>. Combining Roman exiles with Lusitanian and Celtiberian warriors, he defeated Sulla's generals and by 77 BCE controlled much of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Ulterior</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Sertorius</a> ran a rival Roman state with its own senate of exiles and magistrates, while winning Iberian loyalty through fair treatment and personal charisma, aided by a white fawn said to bring him divine messages. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Osca</a> he founded a school where sons of Iberian nobles learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek, partly as hostages. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> and beat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lauron">Lauron</a> in 76 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Worn down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Metellus Pius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, his alliance frayed. In 73 or 72 BCE he was murdered at a banquet by his lieutenant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-perperna-vento">Marcus Perperna</a>, whom Pompey soon defeated and executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> returned to Senate rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 80 BCE – 72 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:58.11%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>80 BCE – 72 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Osca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Quintus Sertorius, Marcus Perperna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sertorian Hispania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-76&amp;focus=sertorian-state" aria-label="Sertorian State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4670 678 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sertorian State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4990 934L5031 967"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Osca. <a href="/atlas?year=-76&amp;focus=sertorian-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
